course: dictionary
lesson: 8028
title: Reformer
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A reformer is a person who works to change something so that it becomes better and fairer. «Reformers like Elizabeth Fry visited prisoners and showed that people could be helped to change.» Here the clues are visited prisoners and helped to change, so a reformer wants to make prisons kinder.
explain: Reformers can change laws, schools, prisons or society as a whole. «Cloth became cheap, but children as young as six worked in mills and mines, which is why reformers fought for laws that sent children to school instead.» Ask yourself: what was wrong, and what did the reformers want to make better?
explain: In church history, the word has a special meaning. «In the 1500s reformers like Martin Luther protested against problems in the Catholic Church; this is the Reformation.» The Reformers were Christian leaders who wanted to renew the Church. When you meet the word, check the topic first, because the topic gives you the clues.
fact: A reformer is a person who works to change something to make it better and fairer.
fact: Reformers can campaign to improve laws, schools, prisons or society.
fact: Elizabeth Fry was a prison reformer who worked to improve prisons.
fact: The Reformers were Christian leaders, such as Martin Luther and John Calvin, who wanted to change and renew the Church.
fact: The Reformation was started by the Reformers and led to the Protestant churches.
exercise: «Reformers like Elizabeth Fry visited prisoners and showed that people could be helped to change.» What is a reformer? | *Someone who works to change something to make it better and fairer | A prisoner who has been locked up | A person who builds mills | A person who sells cloth in a market
exercise: «Children as young as six worked in mills and mines, which is why reformers fought for laws that sent children to school instead.» What did reformers want? | Bigger mines for more children | *Laws that sent children to school instead of mill and mine work | Longer working days in the mills | Prisons with more cells
exercise: In church history, what were the Reformers? | Merchants who sold Bibles in the market | Teachers who taught only in Latin | Soldiers who guarded the church doors | *Christian leaders, such as Martin Luther, who wanted to change and renew the Church
test: «Reformers translated the Bible from Latin into German, English and other languages so ordinary people could read it themselves.» Why did reformers translate the Bible? | *So ordinary people could read it themselves | To make it harder to read | So that only priests could read it | To keep the Bible in one language only
test: Which person is a prison reformer? | A baker who made bread for the village | A sailor who sailed around the world | A king who built a great castle | *Elizabeth Fry, who worked to improve prisons
exam: Which of these best shows a reformer at work? | She built a shop to sell sweets | He raced his bike round the park | *She campaigned to make prisons kinder and fairer | They sat quietly reading a book
